fix: crew labels live in the white, scale with the row, never spill

The hover labels were fixed-size type pinned to percentage offsets. The
wedge they aimed for scaled with the item while the 36px script did
not, so on mid-size screens the words overflowed their slot and landed
across the neighbouring figures — and at the seven-person interlock the
neighbours' panels had consumed those wedges anyway.

Two changes fix both halves.

Placement: the labels move into the white strips the layout guarantees.
Top labels anchor their bottom edge just above the panel's top (y 14%)
and grow upward into the row's padding; bottom labels anchor their top
just below the panel's bottom (y 96%) and grow downward. The strips are
provably clear of every panel at any width: top-labelled items are the
lifted ones, so their unlifted neighbours' colour starts 20% down in
those coordinates, and the lifted neighbours of a bottom label end at
90%. Aligned to the panel's own corner and growing inward, the text
cannot cross into the next slot.

Sizing: each item is now a size container and every label measure is in
cqw — 1% of the item's own width — so the label scales with the row
exactly, and its fit at one breakpoint is its fit at all of them.

Verified by hovering all seven members at 1280, 1440, 1600, 1920 and
2560: every label stays within its own item's horizontal span,
intersects no panel on the row (own or neighbour's), and is visible.
Thirty-five checks, all passing, plus screenshots of both label
variants.
